<h2>How the matter reached the Supreme Court</h2>
<p>The petitioner faced prosecution in Crime No. 441/2025 of Police Station Pachore, District Rajgarh, Madhya Pradesh, for offences under Sections 64(1), 331(2) and 351(3) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 and Sections 3(1)(w)(i), 3(1)(w)(ii) and 3(2)(v) of the SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989. He approached the Supreme Court seeking anticipatory bail. In the meantime, the Madhya Pradesh High Court, in Misc. Criminal Case No. 15763 of 2026, quashed the complaint under its inherent powers on the basis that the parties had arrived at a settlement.</p>
<p>That sequence troubled the Court. In an earlier order of 3 September 2026 it recorded that it was slightly perturbed, given the seriousness of the offence, and called for the record, examining the authorities on which the High Court had acted.</p>

<h2>The limits of quashing on compromise</h2>
<p>The judgment of 26 October 2026 draws the line firmly. The Court held that it could not approve the quashing of a case involving serious offences on a mere settlement. Rape is non-compoundable, and quashing such a prosecution on compromise would exceed the jurisdiction recognised in the settled line of authority — the Court distinguished the decisions the High Court had invoked, including <em>Narinder Singh</em>, <em>Gian Singh</em> and <em>Shambhu Kharwar</em>, as cases concerning disputes of a predominantly personal, civil or commercial character.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The principle is not new, but its restatement is emphatic: the inherent power to quash is not a private compounding mechanism for offences the legislature has made non-compoundable. For heinous offences, the state’s interest in prosecution does not dissolve because the parties have signed a compromise deed.</p>
</div>

<h2>The victim’s statement on coercion</h2>
<p>What gives the judgment its edge is the Court’s direct engagement with the victim. Through video conferencing arranged by the District Legal Services Authority, Rajgarh, she told the Court that she had been coerced into the settlement, was not prepared to settle, and stood by her complaint. The Court directed that the truth of the allegations be examined at trial and recorded its concern in both directions — about false complaints instituted in the hope of a financial settlement, and equally about settlements extracted from complainants through coercion.</p>

<h2>The orders passed</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep">The High Court’s quashing order in Misc. Criminal Case No. 15763 of 2026 was set aside and the trial directed to proceed.</div>
<div class="fstep">The connected order in Criminal Appeal No. 2110 of 2026 was also set aside.</div>
<div class="fstep">The petitioner was directed to be released on anticipatory bail, on conditions to be imposed by the trial court, with obligations to cooperate with the investigation and appear when summoned.</div>
<div class="fstep">The Special Leave Petition was disposed of, with pending applications rejected and no opinion expressed on the merits.</div>
</div>

<h2>What practitioners should take from it</h2>
<p>For the defence, the decision confirms that a quashing petition built on a settlement is a fragile foundation where the offence is grave: even a successful order in the High Court can be undone, and the better-protected route may be to seek anticipatory or regular bail and contest the trial. For complainants, the judgment shows that a settlement signed under pressure is not the end of the road — the Supreme Court was prepared to hear the victim directly and to restore the prosecution. And for both sides, the grant of anticipatory bail alongside the restoration of the case illustrates the modern calibration: liberty is protected through bail conditions, while the allegation itself goes to trial where it belongs.</p>
<p>The observations in the judgment are confined to the question of quashing; the Court made clear that the truthfulness of the allegations remains a matter for the trial court.</p>
